## The idea

A read-through LRU cache: on a **hit** return the cached value; on a **miss** call the loader (the slow thing behind the cache), store the result and count it. Three counters — hits, misses, evictions — give the hit ratio. Stage 2 is about **predicting** the hit ratio: a cyclic scan one key bigger than the cache gets **zero hits** (each key is evicted just before it is needed again); uniform random keys give about **capacity / distinct**; skewed (Zipf) traffic does far better because a few keys get most requests.

## Step by step

1. Keep the usual Dictionary<key, list node> and a LinkedList with the most recent at the front.
2. GetOrLoad: if the key is in the dictionary → hits++, move it to the front, return the value.
3. Otherwise → misses++, call load(key). If the cache is full, remove the last node and evictions++. Add the new value at the front.
4. Stats returns new CacheStats(hits, misses, evictions); HitRatio is computed by the provided record.
5. Workloads: Cyclic yields 0,1,...,n−1 repeatedly; Uniform yields Random.Next(distinct); Skewed picks key k with weight 1/(k+1) using a running-sum array and a binary search.
6. Run(capacity, keys): make a cache, call GetOrLoad for each key, return the stats.

## Worked example

**Input:** capacity 4, cyclic keys 0,1,2,3,4,0,1,...

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| 0,1,2,3 | 4 misses; cache [3,2,1,0] |
| 4 | miss; full → evict 0 (oldest) → [4,3,2,1] |
| 0 | miss — 0 was just evicted; evict 1 → [0,4,3,2] |
| 1 | miss — 1 was just evicted; evict 2 |
| … | every request evicts exactly the key needed next |

**Result:** 0 hits out of 1,000 — with capacity 5 the same scan has only 5 cold misses

## Cost

GetOrLoad is O(1) plus the loader. Memory O(capacity). The workload generators are O(1) per key, except Skewed: O(log distinct) per key for the binary search, and O(distinct) once to build the running sums.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// Read-through LRU cache that counts hits, misses and evictions.
public sealed class CountingLruCache<TKey, TValue> {
    private class Entry {
        public TKey Key;
        public TValue Value;
    }

    private readonly int _capacity;
    private readonly Dictionary<TKey, LinkedListNode<Entry>> _map = new Dictionary<TKey, LinkedListNode<Entry>>();
    private readonly LinkedList<Entry> _order = new LinkedList<Entry>();   // First = most recent
    private long _hits = 0;
    private long _misses = 0;
    private long _evictions = 0;

    public CountingLruCache(int capacity) {
        if (capacity <= 0) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(capacity));
        _capacity = capacity;
    }

    public TValue GetOrLoad(TKey key, Func<TKey, TValue> load) {
        LinkedListNode<Entry> node;
        if (_map.TryGetValue(key, out node)) {
            _hits++;
            _order.Remove(node);                 // a hit is a use: move to the front
            _order.AddFirst(node);
            return node.Value.Value;
        }

        _misses++;
        TValue value = load(key);                // the slow trip the cache exists to avoid

        if (_map.Count == _capacity) {
            LinkedListNode<Entry> victim = _order.Last;
            _order.RemoveLast();
            _map.Remove(victim.Value.Key);
            _evictions++;
        }

        var entry = new Entry();
        entry.Key = key;
        entry.Value = value;
        _map[key] = _order.AddFirst(entry);
        return value;
    }

    // Plain longs are fine: this class is single-threaded.
    public CacheStats Stats {
        get { return new CacheStats(_hits, _misses, _evictions); }
    }
}

public static class Workloads {
    // 0, 1, ..., distinct-1, 0, 1, ... — a scan that repeats.
    public static IEnumerable<int> Cyclic(int distinct, int length) {
        for (int i = 0; i < length; i++) {
            yield return i % distinct;
        }
    }

    // Every key equally likely. Expected hit ratio ≈ capacity / distinct.
    public static IEnumerable<int> Uniform(int distinct, int length, int seed) {
        var random = new Random(seed);
        for (int i = 0; i < length; i++) {
            yield return random.Next(distinct);
        }
    }

    // Zipf-like: key k is picked with weight 1 / (k + 1), so key 0 is the most popular.
    public static IEnumerable<int> Skewed(int distinct, int length, int seed) {
        var random = new Random(seed);

        // runningSum[k] = weight of keys 0..k. Picking a random point in [0, total)
        // and finding which key's range it falls in gives each key its weight.
        var runningSum = new double[distinct];
        double total = 0;
        for (int k = 0; k < distinct; k++) {
            total += 1.0 / (k + 1);
            runningSum[k] = total;
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < length; i++) {
            double point = random.NextDouble() * total;
            int index = Array.BinarySearch(runningSum, point);
            if (index < 0) index = ~index;       // not an exact match: ~index is the next bigger slot
            yield return index;
        }
    }

    public static CacheStats Run(int capacity, IEnumerable<int> keys) {
        var cache = new CountingLruCache<int, int>(capacity);
        foreach (int key in keys) {
            cache.GetOrLoad(key, k => k);        // the "loader" just returns the key
        }
        return cache.Stats;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: What counts as an eviction — does an update?**

No; only inserting a new key into a full cache

**Q: Cost of the counters?**

O(1); the simulator is O(requests)

**Q: A scraper reads the counters while requests run.**

Snapshot under the cache lock, or Interlocked.Read and accept approximation

**Q: A nightly scan of every key.**

Flushes the hot set — scan-resistant policy or bypass

**Q: What do you alert on?**

Relative hit-ratio drop with request rate alongside

